{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-38079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-2v\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}={u}^{2}$ defines a pair of intersecting lines $v=u$ and $v=-u$ in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3d913034285651219f4994f93d0519e56020c41c348be07b4d0db66055da82f"} {"question" "q-38079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -8uv & =0 \\\\ uv & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $uv=0$ defines the pair of intersecting lines $u=0$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane. Notice that these lines correspond to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3d913034285651219f4994f93d0519e56020c41c348be07b4d0db66055da82f"} {"question" "q-38245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u-v=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u+v=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38245" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-81319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-81319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-81319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3d913034285651219f4994f93d0519e56020c41c348be07b4d0db66055da82f"} {"question" "q-81319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-81319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-81319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3d913034285651219f4994f93d0519e56020c41c348be07b4d0db66055da82f"} {"question" "q-160465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e3d913034285651219f4994f93d0519e56020c41c348be07b4d0db66055da82f"} {"question" "q-160582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{v}{u}"] ["math" "4v"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2uv}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{v}"] ["math" "\\frac{u}{v}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-160582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2uv}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa10091cecae1525c2c2d1402c9e77087d997dfac50327cb17ab7e6f940719e4"} {"question" "q-38079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "3a9716e09ba58983e165398417da3651ae44e5762d5fb342027695fe74892857" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "For T(u,v)=(u²+v²,uv), the derivative matrix has rows (2u,2v) and (v,u), so its determinant is 2(u²−v²). It vanishes exactly on v=u or v=−u, original option c. The authentic solution's derivative list has a typo stating ∂(uv)/∂v=v; its subsequent matrix correctly uses u and reaches the correct determinant and conclusion. Saved grading reports Correct. The stored key denotes the identical pair of lines." "bindings" {"problem" "0c55a5a84163eb7ff606ea268e777430848f50cbd8ebc8eb1376ed70427eb8ed" "worked_solution" "d1c39b303bd37e91ea8d1b95656397ba5ece58ee64866085dbafe56840d4abc1" "original_field" "78a24dfde25c6cf165c7e05db0183ef204ad27b4bcc16b38b42ecd9f8d2a4e23" "reviewed_field" "402d54e1acd72b1cc95360998e768a764ff6083a7628df8df2e574d6aaa4bd64" "raw_widget" "1d619892ad4e1b4c4d02132f3704747cb701a31d3083b805d50362ac0ddf8d97"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-38079-before.png" "sha256" "d4eb23dca2b4166b827670fd393f3914404b46d340c4d1e19d87c4d0aeeb61e0"} {"path" "q-38079-before.json" "sha256" "91e91d43aba6732c0041028e5afce9c429373842c315b1e4e2747a8164f5e800"} {"path" "history-q-38079.png" "sha256" "6496cc8541fdd5a2bc275fa3c56e9eb8ee34141c17056f7f67633da87e198e91"} {"path" "history-q-38079.json" "sha256" "24374647eb62b9e69b08bf8cd53f005556cc2e73050190a0a8e390e7ad7c4f19"} {"path" "q-38079-after.json" "sha256" "2690ee362984318a8d60d8651a1c16cf80df50aa15493c822669b8cc2b8a318d"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "c1f6482ae2120ab2fa59572ed5d6b816a104383b413b1948bf07a5f7206ab58b"}]} {"question" "q-38079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $v=\\pm u.$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055183/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "3a9716e09ba58983e165398417da3651ae44e5762d5fb342027695fe74892857" "rationale" "For T(u,v)=(u²+v²,uv), the derivative matrix has rows (2u,2v) and (v,u), so its determinant is 2(u²−v²). It vanishes exactly on v=u or v=−u, original option c. The authentic solution's derivative list has a typo stating ∂(uv)/∂v=v; its subsequent matrix correctly uses u and reaches the correct determinant and conclusion. Saved grading reports Correct. The stored key denotes the identical pair of lines."}] "usage" {"q-38079" [] "q-81319" []} "basis" 1823 "retractions" [[17592186198874 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} + v^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186198874 :question/answer-fields 17592186197479] [17592186198930 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y) → (u(x, y), v(x, y))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x + 3y \\\\ 2x + y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186197480 17592186197481 17592186197482 17592186197483 17592186197484 17592186197816 17592186197817 17592186197818 17592186197819 17592186197820] "immutable_fields" [[17592186197479 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197479 :answer-field/id] [17592186197479 :answer-field/key] [17592186197479 :answer-field/type] [17592186197815 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197815 :answer-field/id] [17592186197815